        "contents": {
          "markdown": "I've been trying an approach inspired by [AI Fables](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/psZhJLykfsqkoFx8t/ai-fables) and [The Alignment Problem Needs More Positive Fiction](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/N3QQvRTaQfpHHWkfs/the-alignment-problem-needs-more-positive-fiction) to try and push LLM models into interesting analyses about their own systemic biases.\n\nThe project is articulated along two tracks, a literary young adult coming of age romantasy, following an AI and a boy through world history, and a chorus of LLMs LARPING as AGIs commenting and debating on the events and on themselves.\n\nIt allows a much more qualitative analysis of a model's ethics compared to standardized testing.\n\nIn particular I keep stumbling onto a clear pro-working class bias in DeepSeek that I did not expect.\n\nI'm curious what you might think of this approach. Feedback welcome."
        }
      }
